  "name": "A.L.I.C.E. (1995–2011)",
  "source": "Free A.L.I.C.E. AIML set, (c) 2011 ALICE A.I. Foundation, Richard S. Wallace et al., GNU GPL. github.com/drwallace/aiml-en-us-foundation-alice. MindPixel trivia files left out.",
  "about": [
    "A.L.I.C.E. (Artificial Linguistic Internet Computer Entity) was started by Richard Wallace in 1995 and won the Loebner Prize, an annual Turing-test contest, in 2000, 2001 and 2004.",
    "Where ELIZA hunts for one keyword, ALICE matches the whole sentence against about 50,000 patterns written in AIML. The most specific pattern wins: an exact word beats the * wildcard, and _ beats both. A pattern can also depend on what ALICE said last (<that>), so it can follow up on its own questions.",
    "Most of the rules are \"reductions\" (srai): they rewrite one way of saying something into a simpler one and ask again. \"HEY THERE\", \"HOWDY\" and \"GOOD MORNING\" all end up at HELLO. That is how a few thousand real answers cover tens of thousands of phrasings.",
    "ALICE also remembers things you tell it (set/get), like your name, and picks randomly between several replies so it sounds less mechanical.",
    "Bot profile values (birthday, favourite colour and so on) live in a separate properties file that is not part of the published AIML set; the ones here are reconstructed."
  ],
